\looseness=-1 We identify conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO) as a systematic failure mode of temporal conditioning in imagination-based actor-critic training. Trained $\dt$-embeddings with multi-rate data exposure reliably induce value overestimation while frozen embeddings never do, revealing co-adaptation between the temporal embedding and the world model as the causal factor. A four-level intervention hierarchy yields two key insights: gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance, and dual critics converge in pure imagination, so Double Q pessimism is structurally ineffective in any system with shared imagined rollouts. For practitioners, we recommend: (1)~validate with frozen-embedding controls before deploying learned embeddings; (2)~monitor NVE as an early warning; (3)~prefer MPC-style re-planning for variable-rate execution. Code and configurations will be released upon acceptance.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\looseness=-1 All experiments use DreamerV3 as the sole world-model architecture; whether CIVO manifests in transformer-based world models or diffusion-based planners remains untested. Observations are proprioceptive and collected in simulation; pixel inputs and real robot transfer have not been evaluated. Our compute budget (${\sim}80$ GPU-hours on a single GPU) restricts the scale of the factorial design; larger sweeps may reveal additional interaction effects. The NVE diagnostic (Algorithm~\ref{alg:civo_detector}) achieves perfect discrimination (AUROC${=}1.000$) but at the recommended $2.0\times$ threshold incurs a 22\% false positive rate, which may limit its utility as an automated stopping criterion without secondary confirmation. The CIVO phenomenon has been characterized in 6-DoF locomotion; the precise complexity boundary at which temporal conditioning becomes harmful remains to be mapped. Future work should investigate whether the co-adaptation mechanism transfers to architectures with different representational inductive biases and validate findings on physical robots under real-world noise and latency.

